---
// What it posts: text-only cards. Cross-model holds (the finder is the AI that didn't write the code,
// the other one double-checks, in Standard and Deep; Quick checks nothing twice); a finding the second look rules out is
// dropped, one it can't confirm is posted marked unconfirmed; "Posted: 2 of 5" is a fair example of what the app counts.
---

<section class="careful" id="posts">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="slab">
      <p class="label">What it posts</p>
      <h2 class="display">Few findings, said plainly.</h2>
      <p class="lede">A reviewer that posts twelve nits gets muted. Lookover is built to post the two that matter.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="grid3">
      <div class="card rise">
        <span class="label">Cross-model</span>
        <h3 class="h4">The other AI reads it</h3>
        <p>
          Code written by Claude is looked over by Codex, and the reverse. Models miss their own habits; a second model
          catches them.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="card rise">
        <span class="label">Double-checked</span>
        <h3 class="h4">Checked before it is posted</h3>
        <p>
          Unless you pick Quick, every finding is re-checked in a fresh session before it reaches you: one ruled out is
          dropped, one left in doubt is marked unconfirmed. Posted: 2 of 5.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="card rise">
        <span class="label">Thread-aware</span>
        <h3 class="h4">It remembers the thread</h3>
        <p>
          On the next push it checks each earlier finding: fixed, still there, or no longer relevant. It only posts what
          is new. An empty review is one line.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
